{"description":"RSF package emission is not atomic despite documentation claiming it is. `eka-core/exchange/write.go` `writeZIP` calls `os.Create(path)` which truncates the destination immediately, and `writeDir` writes files one at a time with `os.WriteFile`. Only entry-name validation happens before any bytes are written, so the documented guarantee holds for a validation refusal and fails for an I/O failure: a crash, disk-full, or permission error leaves a truncated `.ekapkg` or a half-populated directory at the destination, indistinguishable from a complete package without validating contents.","impact":"Exchange packages are the transport between repositories and the workspace, so a partial package surfaces later as a confusing parse error far from the failure site. The snapshot transport already uses a staging-directory-plus-rename discipline for exactly this reason; export does not. Fix: write to a temporary sibling and rename into place on success only, for both emission shapes. Test: simulate a mid-write failure and assert the destination is absent or still holds the previous complete package. Either back the README claim with this behaviour or narrow it to the refusal case it actually describes."}